{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1787082918,"nonce":"UFKJ/s0zSW8gLrOaDoodfBAcrIs=","title":"A Case whose transaction commits after the digest tick has read its window is counted by no window at all, because `started_at` is stamped before the write and the cursor never goes back","message":"**What is wrong.** `digestBucketsSQL` selects on `alert_cases.started_at`, and `started_at` is oto's clock read at the START of a batch's processing — before the transaction that inserts the row. The digest tick reads a closed window at some instant after the boundary. Any Case whose `started_at` falls inside the window but whose transaction had not yet committed when the tick read is invisible to that read, and the tick then writes a digest for the window, which advances the cursor past it. No later window's predicate can reach back, because every later window's predicate starts at or after the boundary that Case is on the wrong side of. The episode is silently uncounted. There is no grace period anywhere in the path.\n\n**The demonstration.** Three facts, each independently correct.\n\n*The predicate is half-open on `started_at`.* `internal/notification/repository/digest.go:72-73`: `AND o.started_at \u003e= $2 AND o.started_at \u003c $3`, bound by `Buckets` (`:101-108`) to the window's `[start, end)`. The half-open shape is deliberate and right — the comment at `:60-65` explains that `\u003c= end` would double-count a boundary episode.\n\n*`started_at` is oto's clock read BEFORE the transaction, not at commit.* `OpenNewCase` sets `StartedAt: p.At.RecordedAt()` (`internal/alerts/domain/lifecycle.go:1077`). `RecordedAt` for an ingested observation is `o.ObservedAt` (`internal/alerts/service/lifecycle.go:1019-1026`), which ingestion stamps as `now` for every alert in the batch (`internal/ingestion/service/process.go:246`), and `now` is `s.clk.Now()` taken once in `plan()` at `:182` — before rejections are written and before `applyChunks` opens a single transaction. Everything between that read and the commit is unbounded latency: decoding, the rejection write, chunking, grouping, the lifecycle machine, `fsync`.\n\n*The cursor is monotonic and derived from the digests themselves.* `lastDigestWindowSQL` (`internal/notification/repository/digest.go:155-158`) is `SELECT max(digest_window_start) ... WHERE subject_kind = 'digest'`. `sweepPolicy` reads it and hands it to `DigestWindows`, which computes `first := d.WindowStart(lastCovered).Add(d.Window)` (`internal/notification/domain/digest.go:202`) and returns `nil` when `first.After(newest)`. So once a window has a digest row, that window is never re-derived — by design, and the design comment at `:140-154` says why: *\"the digest row IS the cursor, so the only state that can exist is state a digest was actually sent for.\"*\n\nPut them together with a ten-minute window. A batch arrives at 12:04:59.95 and `plan()` stamps `now`. Its transaction commits at 12:05:00.30. The `notify.digest` tick fires at 12:05:00.10 (SPEC §G.3 runs it every 60 s, so it lands within the second) and covers `[11:55, 12:05)`. Its `Buckets` snapshot does not contain the uncommitted row. The fold produces a count one lower than the truth, the digest is emitted for window `11:55`, and `max(digest_window_start)` becomes `11:55`. On the next tick `first` is `12:05` and every subsequent window starts later still. The Case sits in the table with `started_at = 12:04:59.95` and no digest anywhere counts it.\n\n**Why it matters.** A digest is oto's answer for the operator who asked not to be told about each firing individually. It is the ONLY thing that operator sees, so an episode a digest drops is an episode nobody is told about — and the drop leaves no row, no log line and no `suppressed_reason`. That is precisely the shape SPEC §B.6 refuses: *\"a suppressed notification and a signal that never fired look identical to the person who was not told.\"* Every other quiet path in this file is accounted for on purpose — a window below the floor `continue`s under a comment arguing why that is not silent suppression, and windows dropped to `MaxDigestBackfill` are logged loudly. This one is not accounted for at all, because nothing knows it happened.\n\nIt is also worse under load, which is when it matters most. The gap between the `plan()` clock read and the commit widens with batch size, chunk count and database contention — so the busier the incident, the wider the vulnerable band at each boundary, and the more episodes are eligible to fall through it.\n\n**Where.** `internal/notification/repository/digest.go:67-79` (`digestBucketsSQL`, the `started_at` predicate), `:96-108` (`Buckets`, the bound), `:130-158` (`lastDigestWindowSQL` and the comment that makes the digest row the cursor) · `internal/notification/domain/digest.go:187-221` (`DigestWindows`, the monotonic `first`) · `internal/notification/service/digest.go` `sweepPolicy` (the `LastWindow` read and the loop over the returned windows) · `internal/alerts/domain/lifecycle.go:1066-1082` (`OpenNewCase`, `StartedAt: p.At.RecordedAt()`) · `internal/alerts/service/lifecycle.go:1014-1026` (`observationTime`) · `internal/ingestion/service/process.go:182` (the single `s.clk.Now()`) and `:246` (`ObservedAt: now`) · `docs/design/SPEC.md` §G.3 (the 60 s `notify.digest` tick) and §B.6.\n\n**Scope.** medium. The mechanism is one predicate and one cursor, but every candidate fix trades against something the current design bought deliberately. A LAG — never digest a window that closed less than G ago — is the smallest change and delays every digest by G. A second predicate on a commit-visible column (there is none on `alert_cases` today) is the only fix that is exact, and adding one is a migration. Re-reading the previous window on each tick and amending the digest breaks the \"a window is covered exactly once\" property the cursor exists to guarantee. Choosing is the work; whatever is chosen, G or the amendment window has to be justified against the observed distribution of the plan-to-commit gap rather than guessed.\n\n**Done when.** A Case whose `started_at` falls in a window is counted by that window's digest, or is counted by a later one, or the drop is recorded where an operator can see it — one of the three, and the file says which and why the other two were refused. A test drives the exact race: a Case row inserted with a `started_at` inside a window that has already been digested, and an assertion about what the operator ends up seeing. If the answer is a lag, the lag is a named constant with the gap distribution it was sized against, and SPEC §G.3's digest row states the added latency. If the answer is that the drop is acceptable, `sweepPolicy` says so at the predicate rather than leaving the next reader to discover that `started_at` is not a commit time.","files":null}]}